/**
  @page guides-boundary-conditions Boundary conditions
  @brief Distinguish prescribed values, natural fluxes, and periodic identifications.

  Prescribed values restrict admissible fields, while prescribed fluxes enter
  the weak equation as loads. This guide follows one mixed-boundary diffusion
  problem, then explains how the same distinction applies to elasticity and
  periodic domains.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-bc-intro Start from the boundary-value problem

  Let @f$\Omega=(0,1)^2@f$. The scalar field satisfies
  @f[
    -\Delta u=0\quad\text{in }\Omega,\qquad
    u=0\quad\text{on the left},\qquad
    \partial_nu=1\quad\text{on the right},
  @f]
  with @f$\partial_nu=0@f$ on the top and bottom. Here @f$n@f$ is the outward
  unit normal and @f$\partial_nu=\nabla u\cdot n@f$. The exact solution is
  @f$u(x,y)=x@f$. Its gradient is horizontal and its right normal derivative is one.

  The word “flux” requires a sign convention. Here it means the prescribed
  normal derivative. A physical diffusive flux defined by
  @f$j=-\nabla u@f$ instead has @f$j\cdot n=-\partial_nu@f$.

  @section guides-bc-mesh 1. Label the boundary regions

  Attributes identify the mesh regions on which boundary conditions or integrals are
  applied.
  This program labels exterior edges by checking both endpoints after
  edge-to-cell connectivity has identified the boundary.

  @snippet PDEs/MixedBoundary.cpp mixed-boundary-mesh

  `fixed`, `loaded`, and `insulated` are local names for integer labels. Other
  meshes may use different values or load their labels from a mesh file. The
  @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "elasticity example" uses the same labeling
  approach for a rectangular beam.

  @section guides-bc-dirichlet 2. Separate essential and natural conditions

  Test functions vanish on the left, where the solution is prescribed. Integration
  by parts gives
  @f[
    \int_\Omega\nabla u\cdot\nabla v\,dx
      =\int_{\Gamma_N}v\,ds,
  @f]
  where @f$\Gamma_N@f$ is the right edge. The boundary contribution vanishes on
  the left because the test function is zero there, and on the top and bottom
  because the prescribed normal derivative is zero.

  @snippet PDEs/MixedBoundary.cpp mixed-boundary-form

  `DirichletBC(...).on(fixed)` restricts the unknown on the selected boundary.
  `BoundaryIntegral(...).over(loaded)` adds the Neumann load. It is subtracted in
  the residual expression, so it appears with a positive sign on the right-hand
  side of the weak equation.

  @subsection guides-bc-dirichlet-nonhomogeneous Prescribe nonzero values

  For a scalar trial function `u`, a known function can supply the boundary value.

  @code{.cpp}
  RealFunction value = [](const Geometry::Point& point)
  {
    return point.x() * point.x();
  };
  auto condition = DirichletBC(u, value).on(fixed);
  @endcode

  Omitting `.on(...)` selects the exterior boundary. Multiple attributes can be
  selected with `.on(first, second)`. The value must have the same range shape
  as the trial field; a two-component displacement can use `Zero(2)`.

  @subsection guides-bc-elimination What essential elimination changes

  With fixed coefficients @f$x_C=g_C@f$, the remaining equations are
  @f[
    A_{FF}x_F=b_F-A_{FC}g_C.
  @f]
  The prescribed values therefore affect both the operator and the load.
  Constraints are imposed by structural elimination.
  These are finite element coefficients; their interpretation depends on the
  space's degrees of freedom.

  The @subpage guides-boundary-elimination "essential constraint elimination reference"
  gives the full projector formulation, sparse storage requirements, and
  implementation costs for callers supplying their own linear systems.

  @section guides-bc-neumann 3. Homogeneous natural conditions

  For this diffusion weak form, omitting a boundary load on an unconstrained
  exterior edge supplies the homogeneous natural condition on the normal derivative. The condition is represented in the weak equation through boundary integration.

  The natural boundary condition follows from the operator and its integration-by-parts formula.

  @subsection guides-bc-neumann-vector Traction in elasticity

  The natural boundary quantity is the traction @f$\sigma(u)n@f$. A vector traction enters through a boundary load, while fixed
  displacement remains an essential condition.

  @snippet PDEs/Elasticity.cpp elasticity-form

  Edges with neither fixed displacement nor a traction load are traction-free
  for this elasticity formulation. See the
  @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "complete beam tutorial" for the model and labels.

  @section guides-bc-periodic 4. Identify boundaries and handle the nullspace

  `PeriodicBC` relates slave and master DOFs across matching boundaries. For a
  scalar P1 grid, the @ref examples-pdes-periodic "periodic Poisson tutorial"
  constructs the pairing using coordinates and the finite element space's DOF
  queries to determine the constrained indices.

  All corner identifications must be consistent. Higher-order spaces also require
  the appropriate edge or face DOFs; pairing only vertices is insufficient.
  The example exercises the native assembly path; backend support should be
  checked before transferring constraints to a different execution backend.

  Periodic Poisson still has a constant nullspace. Its source must satisfy
  @f$\int_\Omega f\,dx=0@f$, and a normalization such as
  @f$\int_\Omega u\,dx=0@f$ selects one solution. Neither identification alone
  nor choosing a direct solver removes that freedom. The same compatibility and
  normalization issue occurs for a connected pure-Neumann Poisson problem.

  @section guides-bc-output 5. Inspect the computed field

  Fixing the left edge removes the constant nullspace, so conjugate gradients
  is suitable for this symmetric diffusion system. The program checks solver
  success before exporting the result.

  @snippet PDEs/MixedBoundary.cpp mixed-boundary-solve

  @image html PDEs/MixedBoundary.png "Computed mixed-boundary solution. Color represents the scalar field; labels identify the prescribed boundary data." width=720px

  The linear field is representable exactly by P1 elements on this affine mesh;
  comparison with @f$u=x@f$ therefore checks boundary labels, the Neumann sign,
  and the algebraic solve without introducing an approximation error from a
  curved exact solution. Other problems still require mesh-refinement checks.

  @section guides-bc-complete Complete program and further reading

  The example is built as the `MixedBoundary` target. See
  @ref PDEs/MixedBoundary.cpp "MixedBoundary.cpp", the
  @ref guides-weak-formulations "weak formulation derivation", and the
  @ref guides-numerical-contracts "numerical contracts" for related details.

  @example PDEs/MixedBoundary.cpp
*/
